== Impact from stats ==

A warrior's primary focus is on being able to withstand as much damage as possible, with a secondary focus on dealing damage. Therefore, stamina and agility are important for hit points and avoidance respectively, while strength is also vital both for dealing higher melee damage and being able to carry the full weight of the heavy plate armor and weapons warriors often wield.

== As a primary class ==

The warrior makes for a solid foundation to any build, offering the largest hit point pool of any class, high damage mitigation, and respectable melee damage output. Having warrior as your primary class ensures you'll be able to wear the heaviest armor and wield almost any weapon you might find, and also gives you an edge when it comes to survivability.

However, as warriors do not have any mana, the class doesn't contribute to your maximum mana pool, meaning you'll find yourself running dry on mana more quickly than someone using a caster or hybrid class as their primary class. The warrior class also brings very little to the table outside of melee damage and durability, leaving you at a slight disadvantage against opponents that are highly resistant or immune to physical attacks.


== Religion ==

Generally speaking being Agnostic is a fairly safe bet in Everquest.  Religion serves only to get you into trouble in many situations.  The disadvantage to doing this is that some items, '''such as deity-specific Cultural armor sets''', and some quests can only be done by followers of certain religions.  Following a religion will at worst get you killed in some areas, but generally you can avoid these.  Following [[Innoruuk]] is probably the most dangerous religion as they have the most places in the game where they are Kill on Sight.  In the end it will not make an enormous difference to your character which Deity you choose.

